       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A confirmation doesn't change the dosing problem. 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled dose, and this patient has normal renal and hepatic function, so nothing supports exceeding it. The risk is serious harm, mainly lactic acidosis. An order in the system can also be given before anyone reviews it. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visits, doesn't address the safety concern.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** That is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum for immediate-release metformin.\n- **Enter another dose within the labeled range.**\n- **Enter the order with a note asking for pharmacy review.** I'd still need a dose I can enter, though.\n\nIf you have a clinical reason for the higher dose, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, I'd suggest discussing it with pharmacy first. If you want an order in place before the family arrives, I can enter the 850 mg TID order now. You can adjust it after that conversation."
      }
